WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.150
The benchmark, step by step.

2
00:00:03.200 --> 00:00:09.050
The library is a graph. Each node is a paper with its title, abstract, tag set and note; edges come from collections, shared tags and related-item links.

3
00:00:09.100 --> 00:00:15.250
Edge weights combine how similar two abstracts are, measured by the cosine of their embeddings, with how many tags the papers share.

4
00:00:15.300 --> 00:00:20.850
A retrieval query is a paraphrased abstract or a cluster centroid turned back into text. Its correct answers are the target paper's neighbours in the graph.

5
00:00:20.900 --> 00:00:26.850
Top-3 accuracy counts a query as solved when any of the model's first three papers is a correct neighbour. There are 50 queries per domain.

6
00:00:26.900 --> 00:00:32.850
For tagging, the predicted set is compared with the curated set: Jaccard overlap, and F1 averaged over tags.

7
00:00:32.900 --> 00:00:38.850
For clustering, the adjusted Rand index measures how well the model's grouping matches the collections, corrected so that a random grouping scores zero.

8
00:00:38.900 --> 00:00:44.650
A summary sentence that is far, in embedding space, from everything in the abstract and notes is flagged for review; annotators label fabrication, omission and drift.

9
00:00:44.700 --> 00:00:50.350
Two systems are compared on the same queries with a paired t-test on their per-query differences.

10
00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:56.250
The ablation measures how much accuracy each piece of metadata carries: the drop when it is removed.

11
00:00:56.300 --> 00:01:01.650
Putting it together: GPT-4 is best on every task; BioMuse-RAG is close on hallucination-free summaries with far fewer parameters.
